<title>UK: UK retail sales rise 0.3 pct in March.</title>
<headline>UK retail sales rise 0.3 pct in March.</headline>
<dateline>LONDON 1997-04-23</dateline>
<text>
<p>The Office for National</p>
<p>Statistics (ONS) released the following seasonally adjusted</p>
<p>data on UK retail sales in March.</p>
<p>    RETAIL SALES VOLUME    MAR     FEB    (FEB PREL)</p>
<p>    Monthly s/adj change   0.3     0.4    (0.5)</p>
<p>    Yr-on-yr change	  4.0     4.3    (4.4)</p>
<p>    Index (base 1990=100)  113.8   113.4 (113.5)</p>
<p>	  3m/3m		  1.0     0.7   (0.6)</p>
<p>	  3m/3m yr ago	 4.4     4.1   (4.0)</p>
<p>Market forecasts had centred on a rise of 0.3 pct on the month and a year-on-year increase of 4.3 percent.  </p>
<p>BY SECTOR (percentage gain, minus indicates fall)</p>
<p>			    m/m	y/y	3m/3m    3m/3m yr ago</p>
<p>Predominantly food   -0.4	1.7	0.5	   2.6 total</p>
<p>pred non-food	   0.2	6.1	1.8	   6.9</p>
<p>non-specialised	 0.8	6.9	0.3	   6.6</p>
<p>Textile,clothing,foot 1.6	6.4	0.8	   6.3</p>
<p>Household goods	-0.7	8.2	2.5	   9.8 Other</p>
<p>stores		   -0.7	3.5	2.9	   4.8</p>
<p>non-store/repair	6.4	2.4     -2.4	  -1.9</p>
<p>The average weekly non-seasonally adjusted value of retail sales in March was 3.19 billion stg, 5.8 percent up on March 96.  </p>
<p>In value terms, retail sales rose by 6.1 percent in the three months to end March on the same period a year earlier.</p>
<p>The ONS said the March 1997 sales period was the five weeks from March 2 to April 5.</p>
</text>
